39" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329682 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329682 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNormalize the vector $f(t)=t-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329682 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1aca67bb-ce76-56b0-b16e-e131cb0a90bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329682 #edb/ref 1074 "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{(t-1)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,({t}^{2}-2t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-{t}^{2}+t)|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-1+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $f(t),$ we divide $f(t)$ by its norm $‖f(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\frac{f(t)}{‖f(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{t-1}{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}(t-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329684 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329684 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42763" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329684 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329684 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V=C[0,2]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,2],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)=t+{t}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329684 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "375ba5e6-834f-57c0-93fa-8f94e8570128" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329684 #edb/ref 1074 "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(t+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}+{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}+\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}+8+\\frac{32}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{256}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{\\frac{256}{15}}=\\frac{16}{\\sqrt{15}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329744 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329744 #edb/ref 1069 "q-43908" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329744 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329744 #edb/ref 1071 "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+4{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+7{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \nCalculate the norm of the vector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ under this inner product." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186329744 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "44115ef9-3207-523e-bdcf-a1809df46edc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329744 #edb/ref 1074 "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $‖v‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3\\cdot 3+4\\cdot ((-2)\\cdot (-2))+7\\cdot (0\\cdot 0)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329747 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329747 #edb/ref 1069 "q-43926" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329747 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329747 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNormalize the vector $f(t)=1-\\sqrt{t}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329747 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1859f87f-98e7-5154-9cca-0bca8773c332" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329747 #edb/ref 1074 "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{(1-\\sqrt{t})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,(1-2\\sqrt{t}+t),\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(t-\\frac{4{t}^{3/2}}{3}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2})|}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =1-\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}.\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $f(t),$ we divide $f(t)$ by its norm $‖f(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\frac{f(t)}{‖f(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1-\\sqrt{t})}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}(1-\\sqrt{t})\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329749 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329749 #edb/ref 1069 "q-43929" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329749 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329749 #edb/ref 1071 "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \nCalculate the norm of the vector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ under this inner product." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186329749 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8baacd11-0f6e-5e47-bcd7-f0798efbe83d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329749 #edb/ref 1074 "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $‖v‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot ((-1)\\cdot (-1))+4\\cdot 4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+2+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2